  <h2 id="victoria">What are the best multi day hikes in Victoria?</h2>
  <p>
    Victoria's two headline multi-day routes both run along the coast rather
    than through alpine country, which makes them a comparatively gentle entry
    point. The Great Ocean Walk links eight hike-in campgrounds from Apollo Bay
    toward the Twelve Apostles, so you can walk one short leg as a first
    overnighter or string several together into a longer trip. A simple
    starter is Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge for one night, then walk back or on
    to Blanket Bay. Two catches: the Elliot Ridge camp sits at the top of a
    steep 800m climb from the Elliot River, and the walk is one-way, so sort out your transport
    before you go.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wilsons Promontory has a chain of coastal campsites including Sealers Cove
    and Refuge Cove. The direct Sealers Cove track from Telegraph Saddle{' '}
    <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/news/2026/09/17/23/34/iconic-sealers-cove-boardwalk-reopens-at-wilsons-prom"
      rel="noopener">reopened in September 2026</a
    > after its boardwalk was rebuilt, but it's still a hilly, Grade 4 walk. For
    a first night at the Prom, the flatter, shorter walk from Tidal River to
    Little Oberon Bay or Oberon Bay is the easier pick.
  </p>
  <p>
    Almost every hike-in campsite in Victoria needs an online booking through
    ParkStay Victoria before you leave home, and that booking doubles as your
    camping permit. A handful of exceptions exist, such as Cathedral Range's
    Farmyard camping area, which Parks Victoria lists as needing no booking or
    fee. Fire rules matter too: Victoria's Country Fire Authority runs a Fire
    Danger Period that can restrict or ban campfires and close parks outright on
    the worst days, so check current ratings for your specific route before
    committing to dates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-victoria/"
      >overnight hikes Victoria guide</a
    > covers verified one-way distances, booking links and a beginner-suitable route
    table for both the Great Ocean Walk and Wilsons Promontory.
  </p>

  <h2 id="new-south-wales">
    What are the best multi day hikes in New South Wales?
  </h2>
  <p>
    NSW splits neatly into two guides because the Blue Mountains has such a
    dense network of its own. West of Sydney, walk-in NPWS campgrounds like
    Acacia Flat near Blue Gum Forest and Kedumba River Crossing sit within a
    couple of hours of the city, and most need an online booking even where
    there's no nightly camping fee. Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest is a
    popular first overnighter: about 5.4km return, but with roughly 570m of
    climb, all of it on the walk out. Carry plenty of water, start the climb
    early before the heat, and wear long pants for leeches.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside the Blue Mountains, NSW has everything from coastal walks to
    multi-week, section-hiked long-distance tracks. Royal National Park's Coast
    Track is best treated as a day walk for now: North Era campground has been
    closed since 2023, there's no campground on the track at Garie, and the
    Garie precinct has had its own closures, so check the current alerts first. Almost every NPWS campsite in the state, including
    free ones, needs an online booking, and for remote country with no bookable
    site, NPWS asks hikers to lodge a free Trip Intention Form instead. NSW's
    statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs from 1 October to 31 March, and rules
    on stoves and open fires tighten further on a declared Total Fire Ban day.
  </p>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/"
      >overnight hikes Blue Mountains guide</a
    > for that region's verified routes and fire rules, and our <a
      href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-nsw/">multi day hikes NSW guide</a
    > for the rest of the state, including the Coast Track, Yuraygir Coastal Walk
    and the Green Gully Track.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tasmania">What are the best multi day hikes in Tasmania?</h2>
  <p>
    Tasmania has arguably Australia's best-known multi-day walking, and also its
    least predictable weather. The Overland Track runs 65km between Cradle
    Mountain and Lake St Clair over about six days and needs a paid booking
    during its 1 October to 31 May season. It's a walk for fit, experienced
    walkers, not a first trip. The Three Capes Track is the friendlier booked
    option: 48km over 4 days and 3 nights with huts and gas cooktops, so you
    don't need a tent or stove, which suits a minimalist kit. The catch is
    cost, at roughly $625 per person (approximate; check the current fee).
  </p>
  <p>
    For a cheap alternative, the Freycinet Peninsula Circuit needs no
    reservation and has no camping fee, and a short overnight at Wineglass Bay
    makes a good first night. It's fuel-stove only and water is limited and
    unreliable, so ask the visitor centre about the Cooks Beach tanks and carry
    enough water for the walk out. Wherever you walk in Tasmania, the weather
    can turn from mild to severe within minutes at any time of year, so pack
    warmer than the calendar suggests and check the Bureau of Meteorology
    forecast close to departure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Full booking details, fees and a route comparison table are in our <a
      href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-tasmania/">multi day hikes Tasmania guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="queensland">
    What are the best overnight hikes near Brisbane, Queensland?
  </h2>
  <figure class="guide-photo">
    <Image
      src={guideImage}
      alt="Main Range National Park seen from farmland at Rosevale, Queensland"
      widths={[360, 720, 1024, 1400]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 680px"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Photo: <a
        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Range_from_Rosevale.jpg"
        rel="noopener">Main Range from Rosevale</a
      > by Shiftchange, public domain (<a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
        rel="noopener">CC0</a
      >), via Wikimedia Commons.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    For a first night near Brisbane, keep it short and simple. <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/daguilar/camping/northbrook-mountain-remote"
      rel="noopener">Northbrook Mountain remote bush camp</a
    > in D'Aguilar National Park is about 50km north-west of the city and roughly
    a 5km walk in along Lawton Road and England Creek Road. It's walk-in or
    bike-in only, holds up to nine people, has no facilities and patchy phone
    reception, and needs a camping permit booked online. Bring all your water
    and a map, as the camp isn't signposted like a campground.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brisbane's two better-known multi-day walks are not a first trip. The <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/main-range/about/scenic-rim-trail"
      rel="noopener">Scenic Rim Trail</a
    > in Main Range National Park is 47km over four days, one-way from Thornton View
    to Cunninghams Gap, and sold as a 3-night camp package. Walkers report the camp
    water tanks can run very low, so don't rely on them. The <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/great-walks-gold-coast-hinterland/about"
      rel="noopener">Gold Coast Hinterland Great Walk</a
    > links Lamington and Springbrook over 54km and three days, with long days of
    around 20km and plenty of leeches; some nights are at private lodges with their
    own bookings. Both are one-way, so you'll need a car shuttle or two cars. Check
    the linked park pages for current fees and closures before you book.
  </p>

