  <blockquote>
    Every state below runs its own booking system and its own fire rules. Treat
    "check the current park page" as step one for any multi day hike, not an
    afterthought.
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="gear">What gear do you need for a multi day hike in Australia?</h2>
  <p>
    The gear side stays mostly the same across states; only the margins change.
    Tasmania and alpine sections of NSW and Victoria call for a warmer <a
      href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
      >sleeping bag temperature rating</a
    > and more <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">sleeping mat R-value</a> than
    a mild coastal walk in Queensland or the Great Ocean Walk. A <a
      href="/guides/2-person-hiking-tent-guide/">two-person hiking tent</a
    > covers most solo and pair setups, and cooking fuel choice depends on the fire
    rules for wherever you're walking, which our <a
      href="/guides/hiking-stove-guide/">hiking stove guide</a
    > covers in detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're assembling a first kit rather than buying piece by piece, our <a
      href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a
    > bundle a matched sleep system and shelter, and <a href="/build-my-kit/"
      >Build My Kit</a
    > fills in whatever you're missing around gear you already own. Run through the
    full <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/"
      >overnight hike packing list</a
    > before any multi-day trip, regardless of state.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    There is no single "best" multi day hike in Australia; there's a best one
    for your state, your experience level and how much booking admin you're
    willing to do. Victoria and the Blue Mountains suit a first overnight trip
    close to a capital city. In Tasmania, Freycinet is the cheap first night and
    Three Capes the comfortable (but pricey) one; the Overland Track is not a
    first trip. Near Brisbane, start with Northbrook Mountain and save the
    Scenic Rim Trail and Gold Coast Hinterland Great Walk for later. Start with
    the regional guide for wherever you're closest to, book early, check the
    park page for fees and closures, and tell someone your plans before you
    go.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the best multi day hike in Australia for beginners?</summary
      >
      <p>
        It depends on where you live. Victoria's shorter Great Ocean Walk legs
        and the Blue Mountains' Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest walk are both
        popular first overnight hikes near a capital city; near Brisbane, try
        Northbrook Mountain bush camp. Tasmania's Three Capes Track suits
        someone ready for a longer booked trip with huts, at roughly $625 per
        person. The Overland Track, Scenic Rim Trail and Gold Coast Hinterland
        Great Walk are not a first trip.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >What are the best multi day hikes in Australia near a capital city?</summary
      >
      <p>
        The Blue Mountains for Sydney, the Great Ocean Walk and Wilsons
        Promontory for Melbourne, and D'Aguilar National Park (Northbrook
        Mountain) for Brisbane are good starting points within roughly two
        hours' drive. Brisbane's Scenic Rim Trail and Gold Coast Hinterland
        Great Walk are also close, but they're long, one-way walks for
        experienced hikers.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Are there good overnight hikes near Brisbane?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. For a first night, Northbrook Mountain remote bush camp in
        D'Aguilar National Park is about a 5km walk in, roughly 50km from the
        city, with a permit booked online and no facilities. The Scenic Rim
        Trail (47km, 4 days) and Gold Coast Hinterland Great Walk (54km, 3
        days) are the bigger options, but they're not a first trip and both
        need a car shuttle.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Do you need to book campsites for multi day hikes in Australia?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Almost always, yes. Every state covered here, Victoria, NSW, Tasmania
        and Queensland, books the large majority of its hike-in campsites
        online, including many free ones. Check the specific park authority's
        page for your route rather than assuming.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Which Australian state has the toughest multi day hikes?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Tasmania's weather is the main wildcard: severe cold, wind and rain can
        hit at any time of year, including summer, on walks like the Overland
        Track. Victoria and NSW have their own challenging sections, such as
        the hilly Sealers Cove track, but Tasmania's alpine exposure is the most consistently
        demanding.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the best time of year for a multi day hike in Australia?</summary
      >
      <p>
        It varies by state and altitude. Tasmania's Overland Track booking
        season runs 1 October to 31 May; NSW and Victoria's fire danger periods
        generally run spring through autumn and affect stove and campfire rules;
        shoulder seasons tend to offer the most stable conditions across the
        mainland states. Check each region's guide and the current fire
        authority page for your dates.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How do fire bans affect multi day hikes in Australia?</summary>
      <p>
        Every state on this list restricts or bans open fires and, on the worst
        days, solid-fuel stoves during its fire danger period, with exact rules
        set by the relevant state fire authority. Rules differ by state and can
        change with little notice, so check your state's current fire authority
        page immediately before a trip rather than relying on last season's
        settings.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
